{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_8980a5cab51960bdcebc524d0a190b9a8dd9984b1f4b494a247a6f4d79ce9f0c", "title": "Feline Frame-Up", "text": "Feline Frame-Up is an animated short film in the Looney Tunes series produced by Warner Bros. Cartoons, Inc and released on February 13, 1954.", "score": 39.7021}, {"id": "hp_doc_f122caa1bbdae9d69caf0c7fc173c8720aad6c96316ed4a15b47f1596b62d250", "title": "Marc Antony and Pussyfoot", "text": "Marc Antony (referred to as \"Marc Anthony\" on his food dish in \"Feed the Kitty\") and Pussyfoot (sometimes called \"Kitty\" or \"Cleo\" in some of the WB animation history books) are animated characters in the Warner Bros. \"Looney Tunes\" and \"Merrie Melodies\" series of five shorts, including \"Feed the Kitty\", \"Feline Frame-Up\", and \"Kiss Me Cat\". Marc Antony is a burly bulldog that is usually brown with a tan belly and black ears, though his coloration varies in some shorts. He bears a close resemblance to Hector the Bulldog, but with thinner back legs and minus the outer fangs. Pussyfoot/Cleo, in contrast, is an extremely cute, blue-eyed black-and-white Tuxedo cat to whom Marc Antony is utterly devoted with motherly passion. The characters seem to be named as an allusion to Marc Antony and Cleopatra, who were lovers detailed in Plutarch's \"Parallel Lives\". Chuck Jones, the creator, has discussed the efforts to maximize the kitten's sheer adorableness. All head and eyes, it is black with a white face and belly and a white tip on its fluffy tail. Fans frequently confuse Marc Antony for Hector, but by right they are not the same.", "score": 15.0504}, {"id": "hp_doc_bcdc5fbae8069108af0d8d10f278ef7aa53cf2b99169023b81a16e9783207f6d", "title": "Duane Capizzi", "text": "Duane Capizzi is an American writer and television producer. He is known for his extensive work in animated series for television, including the Emmy Award-winning \"\" for which he was Co-Executive Producer and Head Writer, and co-developed its follow-up . For Warner Bros Animation, he was writer/producer of the animated series\" The Batman\" as well as its spin-off feature, \"The Batman vs. Dracula\". He wrote the first DC Universe animated feature, \"\" (based on \"The Death of Superman\" saga, and directed by Bruce Timm). Other animated series producing/writing credits include \"Jackie Chan Adventures\", \"Big Guy and Rusty the Boy Robot,\" \"\", and series development on the CG animated \"\" for Sony TV Animation. He was Writer and Story Editor for both animated spin-offs of Jim Carrey movies, \"Ace Ventura Pet Detective\" and . He also wrote and story-edited for several 'Disney Afternoon' TV series including \"Darkwing Duck\", \"Aladdin\", \"TaleSpin\", and \"Bonkers\". He began his career in animation writing scripts for \"\" for Harmony Gold.The series was never produced, but led to writing and story-editing on \"\".", "score": 11.8271}], "remaining_searches": 2}
